        <h1>Lesson I. The Basics</h1>
        <h3>CASES: THE NOMINATIVE AND ACCUSATIVE</h3>
        <p>A <b>case</b> is a form that a word takes based on how it relates to verbs and other nouns in a sentence. For example, take the sentence <i>"She likes me."</i> Since "I" am the one being liked, the word used is "me" instead of "I." If "she" were liked, we would say "her."</p>
        <p>In English, only pronouns are affected by cases; in Latin, nouns and adjectives are as well. Let's look at two basic cases.</b></p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>NOMINATIVE:</b> the agent behind the verb. The one <i>doing</i> something.</li>
            <ul><li><b>The river</b> crosses the valley.</li>
            <li><b>We</b> will resurrect the Empire.</li></ul>
            <li><b>ACCUATIVE:</b> the one affected by the verb.</li><ul>
            <li>The river crosses <b>the valley.</b></li>
            <li>We will resurrect <b>the Empire.</b></li></ul>
        </ul>
        <h3>Declension One</h3>
        <p>Nouns are sorted into <b>declensions</b> based on what letter they end in. Different endings lead to different case endings. Declension I nouns end with <b>-a</b> in the nominative; <b>-am</b> in the accusative.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>stella: </b>star</li>
            <li><b>Roma: </b>Rome</li>
            <li><b>puella: </b>girl</li>
            <li><b>vīta:</b> life</li>
            <li><b>via:</b> way, road</li>
            <li><b>patria:</b> country, fatherland</li>
            <li><b>terra: </b>land</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Other Vocabulary</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><b>non:</b> not</li>
            <li><b>videt:</b> sees</li>
            <li><b>amat:</b> likes, loves</li>
            <li><b>bibit:</b> drinks</li>
            <li><b>vult: </b>wants</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Note that in Latin, words go in this order: nominative, accusative, verb. There are also no words for 'the' and 'a.'</p><p>Let's look at some basic sentences. </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Puella stellas videt. <i>The girl sees the stars.</i> [Lit.: The girl the stars sees.]</li>
            <li>Puella patriam non amat. <i>The girl does not like the country.</i></li>
            
        </ul>
